  "articleBody": "When you're buying a home, a pre-purchase roof inspection is one of the smartest investments you can make. It's not just about what the roof looks like from the curb; it’s about understanding its true condition before you commit. Here in Texas, our roofs go through a lot, so you want to know exactly what you’re getting into. We're all about honest communication and helping you protect your investment, so knowing what a thorough inspection covers and what questions to ask is key to your peace of mind.\n\n### How to Compare Your Options\n-   [ ] **Written Report Delivered Before Deadline** — Ensure you'll receive a comprehensive written report, ideally with photos, well within your contractual inspection period. This is non-negotiable for making informed decisions.\n-   [ ] **Documented Inspection Methods and Limitations** — The report should clearly state how the inspection was performed (e.g., physically walked, drone, visual from ground) and any areas that were inaccessible or not inspected. Understanding limitations helps manage expectations.\n-   [ ] **Assessment of All Components** — Check that the inspection covers not just the roof covering, but also flashing, penetrations, gutters, downspouts, and accessible attic conditions. These are critical for water management.\n-   [ ] **Photographs of Significant Findings** — A picture truly is worth a thousand words. Insist on a report that includes clear photos of any issues or areas of concern. This makes it easier to understand the problem and get repair estimates.\n-   [ ] **Clear Recommendations for Repairs or Further Evaluation** — The report should clearly outline any recommended repairs or suggest when a specialist (like an engineer or another roofer) might be needed for a deeper dive. It shouldn't just list problems but offer next steps.\n-   [ ] **Confirmation of Inspector Independence** — Verify that the inspector is independent and not tied to the seller, agent, or any specific roofing contractor. This ensures you're getting an unbiased assessment.\n-   [ ] **Inspector's Licensing/Certification & E&O Coverage** — Ask about their credentials and if they carry errors and omissions insurance. This protects you if something is missed.\n\n### How to use this checklist\nUse this checklist to evaluate different inspection providers or to review the contents of a report you've received. It helps you ensure you’re getting a thorough, unbiased, and actionable assessment of the roof’s condition before you finalize your home purchase. Ready to get started with a pre-purchase inspection? Contact us at (254) 205-7067 to schedule yours.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-03"
}